Building self-compassion can be a challenging but ultimately rewarding process. One practical approach is to start by acknowledging and accepting your imperfections, rather than trying to focus solely on your strengths. This can involve writing down things you appreciate about yourself, no matter how small they may seem, and then reflecting on why these qualities are important to you. 

Another strategy is to practice mindfulness and self-kindness in your daily life. This can be as simple as taking a few minutes each day to breathe, relax, and remind yourself that it's okay to make mistakes and that you're doing the best you can. 

It's also important to recognize that building self-compassion is a journey, and it's normal for it to take time. If you don't notice any changes, it may be helpful to seek support from a mental health professional, such as a therapist or counselor, who can provide you with personalized guidance and support. 

In terms of knowing when to seek help, it's a good idea to pay attention to your own feelings and needs. If you're consistently struggling with self-doubt, anxiety, or feelings of overwhelm, it may be a sign that you could benefit from additional support. Additionally, if you find that your relationships with others are being negatively impacted by your self-doubt, or if you're having trouble fulfilling your daily responsibilities, it may be time to seek help. 

Remember that seeking help is a sign of strength, not weakness, and that there are many resources available to support you on your journey towards greater self-compassion and self-awareness.

